1
00:00:00,140 --> 00:00:08,180
If you were, uh, looking at the blueprints for a new skyscraper, you'd naturally assume that, you know, some central architect designed the power grid.

2
00:00:08,360 --> 00:00:09,090
Right. Fair point.

3
00:00:09,180 --> 00:00:16,450
You wouldn't expect every single floor to have its own custom-built generator just because, well, just because the electrician for that floor was in a hurry.

4
00:00:16,840 --> 00:00:20,500
No, I mean, that would be an incredibly unsustainable way to build infrastructure.

5
00:00:20,540 --> 00:00:23,020
The maintenance alone would just be paralyzing.

6
00:00:23,200 --> 00:00:25,440
It sounds completely counterproductive. Okay.

7
00:00:25,460 --> 00:00:31,700
But, uh, when we look at our, uh, sources for today's deep dive, it turns out this is happening constantly in the public sector.

8
00:00:31,920 --> 00:00:32,560
Oh, all the time.

9
00:00:33,340 --> 00:00:41,780
So our mission today is to help you, the official running these systems in your ministry spot, two major architectural traps

10
00:00:42,140 --> 00:00:43,800
during the critical assess phase.

11
00:00:44,460 --> 00:00:52,840
We're gonna explore how perfectly rational decisions made by your individual project teams can, you know, silently bankrupt the country's IT budget.

12
00:00:52,900 --> 00:01:02,080
Yeah. And that assess phase, that's exactly where you have the leverage to stop these traps back when they're just, uh, line items in a project plan and still really cheap to change.

13
00:01:02,140 --> 00:01:05,580
Let's start with the first big pitfall: the bespoke trap.

14
00:01:05,660 --> 00:01:10,900
So imagine your ministry is skinning up a new digital portal for issuing business licenses.

15
00:01:11,080 --> 00:01:12,160
Okay. Pretty standard.

16
00:01:12,320 --> 00:01:15,760
Right. And you need an identity authentication function for citizens.

17
00:01:16,280 --> 00:01:20,380
The ideal move is to reuse the existing national identity platform.

18
00:01:20,820 --> 00:01:27,660
But reusing it means, you know, learning their code base, negotiating with the agency that owns it, aligning with their sprint cycles.

19
00:01:27,880 --> 00:01:29,179
Which takes time.

20
00:01:29,800 --> 00:01:36,480
And for the developers inside your specific project, building their own standalone authentication database is just...

21
00:01:36,520 --> 00:01:38,550
Well, it's the path of least resistance.

22
00:01:39,200 --> 00:01:40,399
So it's not just laziness.

23
00:01:40,540 --> 00:01:47,370
No, not at all. Inside the vacuum of that single project, building bespoke is actually the rational choice to meet their deadlines.

24
00:01:47,820 --> 00:01:55,410
But wait, if building it fresh gets them across the finish line faster for, say, a hard launch date set by your minister, what's the actual harm in that?

25
00:01:55,620 --> 00:02:01,600
The harm is the math. Yeah. I mean, it is rational for that single project, sure, but it's completely ruinous for the state.

26
00:02:01,820 --> 00:02:04,930
Mm-hmm. If you multiply that one decision across 10 different ministries-

27
00:02:05,020 --> 00:02:06,760
You're paying for it 10 times.

28
00:02:06,820 --> 00:02:14,260
Exactly. Suddenly, the government is maintaining 10 disconnected identity databases, 10 isolated code bases, and, you know,

29
00:02:14,620 --> 00:02:16,960
accumulating 10 times the technical debt.

30
00:02:17,400 --> 00:02:20,140
You've paid a premium for a function that really should've been built once.

31
00:02:20,360 --> 00:02:25,340
Wow. Yeah. The return on investment for reuse only exists at the whole of government level.

32
00:02:25,360 --> 00:02:30,130
But don't we have standard procurement rules designed to stop exactly this?

33
00:02:30,160 --> 00:02:35,640
Like, if a rule book explicitly mandates open standards and shared services, why doesn't that catch it?

34
00:02:35,660 --> 00:02:38,580
Because a rule book only dictates intention.

35
00:02:38,620 --> 00:02:41,590
Right. It can't alter the laws of project management physics.

36
00:02:41,630 --> 00:02:42,580
What do you mean by that?

37
00:02:42,620 --> 00:02:51,900
Well, a mandate on a piece of paper cannot magically make an external API faster to integrate than a local bespoke build for that specific team.

38
00:02:52,520 --> 00:02:55,960
So optimizing for their immediate deadline, they still just build fresh.

39
00:02:56,000 --> 00:02:59,320
So to actually enforce reuse, you need more than just written rules.

40
00:02:59,420 --> 00:03:08,769
Yes, absolutely. You need a whole of government architectural view, and that has to be backed by a governance board with the actual authority to step in during the assess phase and mandate a shared

41
00:03:08,780 --> 00:03:09,560
building block.

42
00:03:09,600 --> 00:03:12,080
Okay, so let's say that governance board does step in,

43
00:03:12,720 --> 00:03:17,320
or maybe the ministry decides to just buy an off-the-shelf solution instead of building bespoke.

44
00:03:17,460 --> 00:03:20,859
Right, which introduces the second hazard: the vendor-driven trap.

45
00:03:20,920 --> 00:03:28,560
Ah, yes. This happens when a ministry buys a commercial off-the-shelf product, a COTS product, to solve a problem.

46
00:03:29,420 --> 00:03:35,200
It works at first, but over a five-year period, it quietly becomes the architecture itself.

47
00:03:35,210 --> 00:03:37,760
And it happens so gradually. That's what makes it so dangerous.

48
00:03:37,840 --> 00:03:44,000
Right. The internal workflows actually start to mutate to fit the software's proprietary data schemas.

49
00:03:44,260 --> 00:03:54,180
Yes. Like, if the vendor's API has rate limits or it doesn't support a demographic field your ministry needs, your staff start rewriting internal policies or creating manual workarounds just to

50
00:03:54,220 --> 00:03:55,080
keep it all running.

51
00:03:55,140 --> 00:03:57,690
Exactly. You aren't just logged into the software anymore.

52
00:03:57,720 --> 00:04:01,900
Your actual government operations are dictated by the vendor's database structure.

53
00:04:01,920 --> 00:04:06,720
At that point, the only people who actually understand how your architecture functions work for the vendor.

54
00:04:07,100 --> 00:04:10,840
So is the takeaway here that buying from vendors is inherently a mistake?

55
00:04:10,940 --> 00:04:13,030
Should we just be building everything in-house?

56
00:04:13,440 --> 00:04:16,980
Oh, no, not at all. Buying is often the exact right choice.

57
00:04:17,019 --> 00:04:21,640
The trap isn't the vendor itself. The trap is surrendering architectural control over time.

58
00:04:21,840 --> 00:04:23,020
Which brings us to the safeguard.

59
00:04:23,660 --> 00:04:28,780
The sources outline four critical questions you must ask at the assess phase to catch both of these traps.

60
00:04:29,540 --> 00:04:30,360
Let's hit the first one.

61
00:04:30,440 --> 00:04:31,800
Sure. Question one:

62
00:04:32,440 --> 00:04:35,000
Does a shared building block already exist?

63
00:04:35,800 --> 00:04:40,620
So checking for existing national platforms before writing a single line of code makes sense.

64
00:04:41,040 --> 00:04:41,680
Question two.

65
00:04:42,340 --> 00:04:46,510
Is the data stored to an open standard or a vendor's proprietary format?

66
00:04:46,740 --> 00:04:49,200
Which prevents that workflow mutation we just talked about.

67
00:04:49,380 --> 00:04:50,960
Right. You have to retain data portability.

68
00:04:51,520 --> 00:04:56,770
Question three: If this supplier doubled their price tomorrow, could we replace them within two years?

69
00:04:57,060 --> 00:05:00,900
Two years. That is a brutal test of interoperability.

70
00:05:00,920 --> 00:05:01,280
It is.

71
00:05:01,940 --> 00:05:09,640
But if your data is trapped in their format, migration would take five years, which means you have absolutely zero leverage in contract negotiations.

72
00:05:10,020 --> 00:05:12,180
That is a great point. And the final question.

73
00:05:12,500 --> 00:05:13,080
Question four:

74
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:17,360
Is the sourcing choice deliberate? Whether you build,

75
00:05:18,020 --> 00:05:25,350
buy, share, or sandbox, meaning prototyping in a safe environment first, the decision just has to be intentional.

76
00:05:25,820 --> 00:05:31,240
So to sum it up, the bespoke trap duplicates effort, and the vendor-driven trap surrenders control.

77
00:05:31,280 --> 00:05:36,940
Both are incredibly costly but entirely avoidable if you flag them early at the assess phase in your program.

78
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:40,300
Exactly. Catch them while the architecture is still cheap to change.

